Let $P(x) = x^3 + 2x^2 + 2x + c$ and $Q(x) = x^2 + bx + b$. Since $Q(x)$ is a factor of $P(x)$, we can write $P(x) = (x+a)Q(x)$ for some constant $a$.
Then
\begin{align*} x^3 + 2x^2 + 2x + c &= (x+a)(x^2 + bx + b) \\ &= x^3 + bx^2 + bx + ax^2 + abx + ab \\ &= x^3 + (a+b)x^2 + (b+ab)x + ab \end{align*}
Comparing coefficients, we have
\begin{align*} a+b &= 2 \\ b+ab &= 2 \\ ab &= c \end{align*}
From the first equation, $a = 2-b$. Substituting this into the second equation, we get
\begin{align*} b + (2-b)b &= 2 \\ b + 2b - b^2 &= 2 \\ -b^2 + 3b - 2 &= 0 \\ b^2 - 3b + 2 &= 0 \\ (b-1)(b-2) &= 0 \end{align*}
Thus, $b=1$ or $b=2$.

If $b=1$, then $a = 2-b = 2-1 = 1$. Then $c = ab = 1 \cdot 1 = 1$.
If $b=2$, then $a = 2-b = 2-2 = 0$. Then $c = ab = 0 \cdot 2 = 0$.

If $b=1$, then $x^2 + x + 1$ is a factor of $x^3 + 2x^2 + 2x + 1$. We can perform polynomial long division to check:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cc cc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x} & +1 \\
\cline{2-5}
x^2+x+1 & x^3 & +2x^2 & +2x & +1 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^3} & +x^2 & +x \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & x^2 & +x & +1 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & x^2 & +x & +1 \\
\cline{3-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
\end{array}
\]
So $x^3 + 2x^2 + 2x + 1 = (x+1)(x^2+x+1)$.

If $b=2$, then $x^2 + 2x + 2$ is a factor of $x^3 + 2x^2 + 2x + 0 = x^3 + 2x^2 + 2x$. We can perform polynomial long division to check:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cc cc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x} \\
\cline{2-5}
x^2+2x+2 & x^3 & +2x^2 & +2x & +0 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^3} & +2x^2 & +2x \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & 0 & 0 & +0 \\
\end{array}
\]
So $x^3 + 2x^2 + 2x = x(x^2+2x+2)$.

Therefore, the possible values of $b$ are 1 and 2.

